---
title: Hasselback potato gratin
servings: 4
yield: 4 to 6 servings
tags: [vegetarian, gluten-free, thanksgiving, holiday]
time: 1 hour 45 minutes
source: Hasselback potato gratin by Kelly Brant, Arkansas Democrat-Gazette, in the Jefferson City News Tribune, https://www.newstribune.com/news/2024/nov/20/hasselback-potato-gratin-a-creamy-cheesy-side/
---

Heat the oven to 400°F with a rack in the middle. Butter a #2-quart baking dish{} with @unsalted butter{}.

Mix the @Gruyère{3%oz}(finely grated; or Comté) and @Parmesan{2%oz}(Parmigiano-Reggiano, finely grated) in a #large bowl{}. Move about a third of it to a #small bowl{} for the top.

Add the @heavy cream{2%cups}, @fresh thyme{1%tbsp}(roughly chopped) and @garlic{2%cloves}(minced) to the large bowl. Season well with @kosher salt{} and @black pepper{}.

Slice the @russet potatoes{3%lb}(peeled; 3 to 3 1/2 lb) 1/8 inch thick on a #mandoline{}. Toss them in the cream, pulling apart any slices that stick.

Stand the slices on edge in the dish in tight stacks, working from the outside in, until the dish is packed. Slice another potato if there are gaps.

Pour in cream mixture until it comes halfway up the potatoes. Cover tightly with #foil{} and bake ~{30%minutes}.

Uncover and bake ~{30%minutes} more, until the top is pale gold.

Scatter over the saved cheese and bake ~{30%minutes} more, until deep golden and crisp. Rest a few minutes before serving.
